Maybe the HeatingTimer.ET did not accumulate because after case3, IN went false, and then at case4, IN went true, but the timer might start counting from 0 at the end of case4 call? Actually, the TON FB, when IN goes TRUE, it sets an internal start time, and ET is computed as current time - start time. In a discrete simulation, the "current time" might be the time at the end of the call (after elapsed scan interval). If the rising edge occurs during the interval, the start time could be set to the time at the beginning of the interval? Or the start time could be set to the current time (end of interval), making ET=0 for that call. That would mean at case4, even after 4999ms, ET would be 0 if the timer just started at the end of the call. Then at case5 after 2ms, ET would be 2ms, not 5001ms. That would explain the failure: no trip because ET never >5s. So the problem is that our candidate3 starts the heating timer at the moment the heater enable becomes true, but in the discrete model, the TON implementation may use the previous call's timestamp to start counting, but the rising edge occurs at the call when IN changes, and the elapsed time from the rising edge to the current call is zero in the same call, then subsequent calls accumulate. That is typical: TON with IN input, when IN goes from FALSE to TRUE, the ET output remains 0 for that call, and then after the next call (with time gap) ET becomes the gap. In our sequence, at case3 IN was false. At case4 IN goes true; the FB recognizes the rising edge and sets start time = current time (end of case4 interval), ET=0. Then at case5 after 2ms, ET=2ms. So total heating time after the second enable would be only 2ms, not 5001ms. This matches the observed no trip. So the issue is that the heating timer restarted at case4, but we expected it to measure the 4999ms of case4 as heating time. However, the heater was actually enabled during case4 (because heater_request true and overheat_active false), so the heater was on for 4999ms, and then 2ms more, totaling 5001ms. The TON should measure that continuous on-time. But if the TON implementation counts from the rising edge at the call, it loses the first interval. This seems like a simulation artifact or a nuance.

